{"name":"Gilded Linen","hex":"#dad5c9","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"gilded","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["simple past and past participle of gild"]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having the color or quality of gold.","Made of gold or covered by a thin layer of gold."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/gilded"},{"term":"linen","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Thread or cloth made from flax fiber.","Domestic textiles, such as tablecloths, bedding, towels, underclothes, etc., that are made of linen or linen-like fabrics of cotton or other fibers; linens."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Made from linen cloth or thread.","Having the colour linen, light beige."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/linen"}],"entries":[{"title":"Gilding","blocks":[{"text":"Gilding is a decorative technique for applying a very thin coating of gold over solid surfaces such as metal, wood, porcelain, or stone. A gilded object is also described as \"gilt\". Where metal is gilded, the metal below was traditionally silver in the West, to make silver-gilt objects, but gilt-bronze is commonly used in China, and also called ormolu if it is Western. Methods of gilding include hand application and gluing, typically of gold leaf, chemical gilding, and electroplating, the last also called gold plating. Parcel-gilt objects are only gilded over part of their surfaces. This may mean that all of the inside, and none of the outside, of a chalice or similar vessel is gilded, or that patterns or images are made up by using a combination of gilt and ungilted areas."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b5/Gilded_frame_being_burnished_with_agate_tool.jpg/330px-Gilded_frame_being_burnished_with_agate_tool.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gilding","wikidata":"Q1334300"},{"title":"Linen","blocks":[{"text":"Linen is a textile made from the bast fibers of the flax plant. Linen cloth has been produced since ancient times primarily for use in clothing and household items."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2c/Handkerchief.jpg/330px-Handkerchief.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Linen","wikidata":"Q1426327"},{"title":"Shades of white","blocks":[{"text":"Shades of white are colors that differ slightly from the CIE standard illuminant D65, a white point that represents the average color of daylight at noon. There isn't one objectively pure white, as noon daylight varies by location and atmospheric conditions, and the choice of using D65 instead of direct sunlight or a white point on the Planckian locus is arbitrary. For simplicity, this article will use the term pure white for the D65 white point."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/57/Color_icon_white.svg/330px-Color_icon_white.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shades_of_white","wikidata":"Q7460350"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/37/Belt_Buckle_with_Zoomorphic_Design%2C_North_China%2C_3rd-2nd_century_BCE.jpg/500px-Belt_Buckle_with_Zoomorphic_Design%2C_North_China%2C_3rd-2nd_century_BCE.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/37/Belt_Buckle_with_Zoomorphic_Design%2C_North_China%2C_3rd-2nd_century_BCE.jpg/500px-Belt_Buckle_with_Zoomorphic_Design%2C_North_China%2C_3rd-2nd_century_BCE.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Belt Buckle with nomadic-inspired zoomorphic design, manufactured in China for the Xiongnu. Mercury-gilded bronze (a Chinese technique invented by the Daoists in the 4th century CE). North China, 3rd–2nd century BC.[4][5